<p class="ui center aligned" style="margin-top: 0.5em; font-size: 0.95em; color: rgb(10, 10, 10); max-width: 800px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;">
  This image, captured by the James Webb Space Telescope, is one of the deepest views of the universe ever taken. 
  Known as Webb’s First Deep Field, it reveals thousands of galaxies in a tiny patch of sky no larger than a grain of sand held at arm’s length. 
  Some of the light in this image has travelled for over 13 billion years, allowing us to look back in time to when the universe was in its infancy. 
  The warped shapes of some galaxies are caused by gravitational lensing, where massive objects bend light and magnify distant galaxies behind them.
</p>
